§6 — THE THREE SEARCHES. 2026-08-04T06:33:01Z

THE FINDING IS ABOUT MY OWN SUITE. The first version passed 15/15 and FOUR OF FIVE seeded
bugs escaped it:
  SEED 1 rank by ordinal instead of cosine    -> 15/15 PASS (should have been caught)
  SEED 2 containment instead of range overlap -> 15/15 PASS
  SEED 3 match-count floor drops citations    -> caught
  SEED 4 drop the empty-query guard           -> 15/15 PASS
  SEED 5 drop the explicit user_id predicate  -> 15/15 PASS

DIAGNOSIS, measured not guessed: the fixture was 490 characters against a 1200-char chunk
budget, so it produced exactly ONE section. Every ordering assertion was comparing a
one-element list with itself. And every anchor was a single verse, so overlap and
containment gave identical answers.

FIXED: the fixture is now 25 paragraphs / 5,066 chars / 5 sections, and it cites Romans 8 at
CHAPTER grain (45008001-45008999) as well as 8:28, so a wide anchor exists that a
single-verse query must still find. Two PRECONDITIONS now fail loudly if a future edit
shrinks the fixture below what can discriminate -- >=5 sections, and >=1 wide anchor.
Two rows would catch a wrong ORDER BY only about half the time; five makes accidental
agreement vanishingly unlikely. Seed 1 was re-run THREE times to confirm determinism.

